    <h1>From a financial question to an inspectable result.</h1>
    <p class="study-product">
      Zoidberg Terminal <span class="badge">Waitlist</span> &middot;
      <a href="https://zoidbergterminal.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener">zoidbergterminal.com&nbsp;&rarr;</a>
    </p>

    <section class="study-block">
      <h2>The task</h2>
      <p>
        Someone wants to understand a public company. Today that means a filings site, a charting
        tool, a prediction market, a macro series and five open tabs &mdash; then the work of
        remembering which number came from where, and how old each one was.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="study-block">
      <h2>The information required</h2>
      <p>
        What the company filed and when. Who has been buying or selling it. What prediction markets
        are pricing about it. What the macro series underneath it actually says. Four different
        institutions, four different interfaces, four different update cadences &mdash; and no
        shared notion of how fresh any of it is.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="study-block">
      <h2>What it does</h2>
      <p>
        Type a ticker and get a grid of panels drawn from those sources at once. Every call routes
        through Pipeworx rather than to a provider directly, which is what makes the next part
        possible: <strong>every number on screen carries the source it came from and the moment it
        was read</strong>. Where a source published nothing, the terminal says so instead of
        showing a zero &mdash; an absent figure and a figure of zero are different facts, and
        conflating them is how a research tool misleads quietly.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="study-block">
      <h2>The result</h2>
      <p>
        It works and it is in daily use. Because every call is attributed and timed, a result can be
        handed to someone else and checked rather than taken on trust &mdash; which is the whole
        point of putting this infrastructure underneath it.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="study-block study-limits">
      <h2>What remains limited</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>It is early, and it is narrow on purpose.</strong> It is not a trading terminal:
        there is no execution and no portfolio accounting. It is not real&#8209;time for equities,
        because public sources cannot honestly provide that &mdash; crypto and prediction markets
        are live, equities are end&#8209;of&#8209;day unless you bring your own market-data key.
        And it is not advice. It shows what filings and markets say; what that means is yours.
      </p>
      <p>Access is by waitlist while it is still moving quickly.</p>
    </section>

